(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-cardiac-physiology-quiz"]={"title":"Cardiac Physiology — Physiology Exam 2","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why is tetany not possible in cardiac muscle, unlike skeletal muscle?","choices":["Cardiac muscle has a much longer duration of both action potential and contraction compared to skeletal muscle","Cardiac muscle contracts too weakly to ever generate a palpable pulse without external pacing assistance","Cardiac muscle cannot generate action potentials at all, relying entirely on mechanical stretch to trigger contraction","Cardiac muscle has no troponin or tropomyosin, relying on a calcium-independent contraction mechanism instead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cardiac muscle has a longer duration of both action potential and contraction compared to skeletal muscle, which (combined with its long absolute refractory period) prevents tetany from occurring. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What structurally connects individual cardiac muscle fibers into a functional syncytium, and what electrically separates the atrial and ventricular syncytia?","choices":["Tight junctions at intercalated disks connect fibers; loose connective tissue only partially separates the atrial and ventricular syncytia","Gap junctions at intercalated disks connect fibers within a syncytium; a fibrous insulator (skeleton) separates the atrial and ventricular syncytia","Desmosomes alone provide electrical connection between fibers; a bony ring of cartilage separates the two syncytia","The atrial and ventricular syncytia are connected by gap junctions directly through the A-V valve leaflets themselves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Gap junctions at intercalated disks provide low-resistance electrical connections between cardiac muscle fibers, forming atrial and ventricular syncytia; a fibrous insulator (the cardiac skeleton) separates the two syncytia so that impulses must pass through the specialized conduction system. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What two features distinguish the ventricular muscle action potential from the skeletal muscle action potential?","choices":["Skeletal muscle APs show a ~150 ms plateau caused by increased K+ conductance, longer than cardiac muscle's brief 2 ms AP","Cardiac muscle APs show a ~200 ms plateau caused by decreased K+ conductance and increased Ca2+ conductance, versus skeletal muscle's 1-5 ms AP","Both types of muscle have an identical AP duration of about 5 ms, driven purely by fast Na+ channel kinetics","Cardiac muscle APs are shorter than skeletal muscle APs and depend entirely on fast Na+ channels with no calcium involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ventricular muscle action potential shows a roughly 200 ms plateau caused by decreased K+ conductance and increased Ca2+ conductance (an important calcium source for contraction) — features not seen in the skeletal muscle AP, which lasts only 1-5 ms. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"How long does the absolute refractory period last in the ventricles, and what does it prevent?","choices":["0.25-0.30 seconds, preventing tetanus in heart muscle","0.001 seconds, with no functional significance","0.15 seconds, identical to the atrial refractory period","5 seconds, preventing all contraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ventricular absolute refractory period lasts about 0.25-0.30 seconds (roughly matching the duration of contraction), preventing the heart muscle from being re-excited and thus preventing tetanus; the atrial refractory period is shorter, about 0.15 seconds. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What triggers ryanodine receptor opening and Ca2+ release from the SR in cardiac muscle EC coupling?","choices":["Ca2+ entering through voltage-gated L-type Ca2+ channels in the T-tubule binds the ryanodine receptor, causing it to open and release a large amount of additional Ca2+ (calcium-activated calcium release)","Direct mechanical deformation of the SR membrane by the T-tubule voltage sensor, physically coupled to the ryanodine receptor without any calcium involvement","ATP binding directly to the ryanodine receptor following its release from nearby mitochondria during periods of sustained high-frequency cardiac stimulation","Voltage alone acting directly on the ryanodine receptor through a mechanical protein linkage, with no calcium involvement at all, exactly as in skeletal muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"In cardiac EC coupling, an action potential opens voltage-gated L-type Ca2+ channels in the T-tubule, allowing Ca2+ to enter the sarcoplasm; this Ca2+ binds to the ryanodine receptor, causing it to open and release a much larger amount of Ca2+ from the SR — calcium-activated calcium release (CACR). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How do cardiac T-tubules compare to skeletal muscle T-tubules in volume, and what is the functional consequence?","choices":["Cardiac T-tubules are about 25 times smaller than skeletal muscle T-tubules, and cardiac contraction strength depends only on intracellular SR calcium stores","Cardiac T-tubules have about 25x greater volume than skeletal muscle T-tubules, and (unlike skeletal muscle) cardiac contraction strength depends on extracellular calcium concentration","T-tubule volume is identical between the two muscle types, and both rely equally heavily on extracellular calcium for contraction strength","Extracellular calcium has no effect on cardiac contraction strength, which depends entirely on calcium stored within the sarcoplasmic reticulum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cardiac T-tubules have about 25 times greater volume than skeletal muscle T-tubules and store calcium (bound to negatively charged mucopolysaccharides); because cardiac muscle relies on extracellular calcium entering via the T-tubule (not just SR release, as in skeletal muscle), the strength of ventricular contraction depends on extracellular calcium concentration. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What are the two main phases of systole and the four sub-phases of diastole in the cardiac cycle?","choices":["Systole: isovolumic contraction and ejection; diastole: isovolumic relaxation, rapid inflow, diastasis, and atrial systole","Systole has only one phase, ejection, lasting the entire 0.3 seconds of ventricular contraction each cycle","Systole: rapid inflow and diastasis; diastole: isovolumic contraction, ejection, and atrial systole","There are no distinct phases within systole or diastole, since ventricular pressure changes continuously and smoothly"],"answer":0,"exp":"Systole consists of isovolumic contraction and ejection; diastole consists of isovolumic relaxation, rapid inflow, diastasis, and atrial systole. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What causes the first and second heart sounds, and which valve types are responsible for each?","choices":["Heart sounds are unrelated to valve closure, arising instead from turbulent blood flow through partially open valves","S1 (soft, \"lub\") is caused by closure of the semilunar valves; S2 (rapid snap, \"dub\") is caused by closure of the A-V valves","Both heart sounds are caused by simultaneous closure of all four cardiac valves at the same instant","S1 (soft, \"lub\") is caused by closure of the A-V valves; S2 (rapid snap, \"dub\") is caused by closure of the semilunar valves"],"answer":3,"exp":"The first heart sound (S1, soft closure, \"lub\") is caused by closure of the A-V valves (mitral and tricuspid); the second heart sound (S2, rapid snap, \"dub\") is caused by closure of the semilunar valves (pulmonary and aortic). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Do papillary muscles help close the heart valves?","choices":["Papillary muscles have no relationship to the cardiac valves at all, functioning only to anchor the ventricular walls during contraction","They only function during diastole, relaxing to allow the A-V valve leaflets to open fully for ventricular filling","Yes, they are the primary mechanism of valve closure, contracting forcefully just before systole to pull the leaflets fully shut","No — they contract when the ventricles contract, but their role is to prevent the valve vanes from bulging back into the atria, not to help the valves close"],"answer":3,"exp":"Papillary muscles contract when the ventricles contract, but they do not help the valves close — their function is to prevent the AV valve vanes (via chordae tendineae) from bulging backward into the atria during ventricular contraction. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How is ejection fraction (EF) calculated, and what does it clinically assess?","choices":["EF = EDV / SV (end-diastolic volume divided by stroke volume), used to estimate total blood volume","EF = ESV / EDV (end-systolic volume divided by end-diastolic volume), used clinically to estimate heart rate","EF has no clinical relevance, since it varies too unpredictably between healthy individuals to be diagnostically useful","EF = SV / EDV (stroke volume divided by end-diastolic volume), used clinically to assess cardiac pumping capability"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ejection fraction (EF) = stroke volume (SV) / end-diastolic volume (EDV); stroke volume itself equals EDV minus end-systolic volume (ESV). EF is used clinically (often measured by echocardiogram) to assess cardiac pumping capability. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What causes the incisura (dicrotic notch) seen on the aortic pressure curve, and when is it absent?","choices":["It has no relationship to valve function, arising instead purely from turbulent blood flow circulating within the aortic arch","It is caused by atrial contraction pushing blood forward into the ventricle, and it is absent whenever atrial fibrillation is present","It is caused by a brief period of backward flow just before aortic valve closure followed by sudden cessation of backflow; it is absent with aortic regurgitation","It reflects mitral valve opening at the start of diastole, and is characteristically absent in cases of severe mitral stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The incisura (dicrotic notch) occurs with aortic valve closure, caused by a short period of backward flow immediately before valve closure followed by sudden cessation of that backflow; it is absent in aortic regurgitation, where the valve doesn't close properly. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What causes the \"c wave\" seen in the atrial pressure curve?","choices":["Slow, continuous venous return filling the atria during ventricular systole","Atrial contraction itself, pushing the a wave upward just before the c wave","Contracting ventricles causing the A-V valves to bulge backward toward the atria","The rapid opening of the semilunar valves at the start of ventricular ejection"],"answer":2,"exp":"The atrial pressure curve's \"c wave\" is caused by contracting ventricles pushing the A-V valves to bulge backward toward the atria; the \"a wave\" reflects atrial contraction, and the \"v wave\" reflects slow atrial filling followed by rapid ventricular filling once the A-V valves open. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is preload, and what is it proportional to?","choices":["The intrinsic contractility of the myocardium, independent of any change in ventricular volume or filling pressure","The degree of passive tension on the ventricular muscle at the end of diastole, proportional to end-diastolic volume (EDV)","The pressure the heart must pump against during systole, proportional to mean arterial pressure at the aortic valve","The heart rate at rest, proportional to background parasympathetic tone acting on the SA node"],"answer":1,"exp":"Preload of the left ventricle is the degree of passive tension on the muscle when it begins to contract at the end of diastole, proportional to end-diastolic volume (EDV) — greater preload increases actin-myosin interdigitation, producing a stronger contraction (Frank-Starling mechanism). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is afterload, and what is it proportional to?","choices":["The volume of blood remaining in the atria after ventricular contraction has finished","The heart rate alone, determined entirely by intrinsic SA node discharge frequency","The passive stretch on the atrial wall at end-diastole, proportional to atrial filling pressure","The pressure the heart must work against to eject blood during systole, proportional to mean arterial pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Afterload of the left ventricle is the pressure the heart must work against to eject blood during systole (assuming a normal aortic valve), proportional to the average (mean) arterial pressure. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What fraction of the oxygen in blood does the heart typically extract, compared to the average tissue?","choices":["The heart extracts about 20% of blood oxygen, slightly less than the average resting tissue","The heart uses 25% of blood oxygen, exactly identical to the average tissue's extraction rate","The heart uses nearly 100% of blood oxygen continuously, leaving almost none for venous return","The heart uses 70-75% of blood oxygen, compared to only about 25% for the average tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"The heart uses 70-75% of the oxygen in blood passing through it, compared to the average tissue which uses only about 25% — reflecting the heart's very high, near-maximal oxygen extraction and its heavy reliance on adequate coronary blood flow. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the primary fuel source used by the heart for energy production?","choices":["Ketone bodies exclusively, with no other fuel used","Fatty acids","Glucose exclusively","Amino acids exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fatty acids are the primary fuel source for the heart; oxygen consumption by the heart is directly related to the amount of tension development, and is increased by increased afterload, heart size, contractility, and heart rate. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How is stroke work calculated?","choices":["Stroke work = heart rate x contractility index","Stroke work = stroke volume x aortic pressure","Stroke work = end-diastolic volume x heart rate","Stroke work = ejection fraction alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Stroke work is calculated as stroke volume (SV) multiplied by aortic pressure, reflecting the mechanical work performed by the ventricle during each ejection. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is myocardial contractility, and what factors increase it (positive inotropic effect)?","choices":["A measure of heart rate alone, entirely unrelated to sarcomere length, actin-myosin overlap, or the force generated during any individual contraction","The passive stretch of the ventricle at end-diastole/actin-myosin interdigitation; increased only by preload and unaffected by any neural or hormonal factors","Contractility cannot be modified by any pharmacologic, neural, or hormonal factor whatsoever once the heart has reached full physiological maturity in adulthood","The ability to develop force at a given sarcomere length/actin-myosin interdigitation; increased by increased heart rate, sympathetic stimulation, cardiac glycosides, and increased extracellular calcium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Myocardial contractility is the ability to develop force at a given sarcomere length (a given amount of actin-myosin interdigitation); it is increased (positive inotropic effect) by increased heart rate, sympathetic stimulation, cardiac glycosides (digoxin, digitalis), and increased extracellular calcium concentration. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which of the following is used clinically as an estimate of increased contractility?","choices":["Contractility cannot be reliably estimated by any current clinical measure or imaging technique","Increased ejection fraction (SV/EDV), increased peak systolic pressure, and increased dP/dT","Decreased ejection fraction (SV/EDV) and a slower rate of pressure rise (dP/dT)","Decreased peak systolic pressure and a prolonged isovolumic contraction time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Increased contractility is estimated by increased ejection fraction (SV/EDV), increased peak systolic pressure, and increased dP/dT (or (dP/dT)/P) — the rate of pressure rise during isovolumic contraction. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What does the Frank-Starling mechanism (normal cardiac function curve) demonstrate, and is it dependent on innervation?","choices":["It shows that cardiac output is fixed regardless of venous return, and is entirely dependent on intact sympathetic and parasympathetic innervation","It demonstrates that contractility alone determines cardiac output, completely independent of any change in venous return or ventricular filling volume","It only applies when the heart is surgically denervated, ceasing to function properly once sympathetic and parasympathetic connections are restored","It is based on the length-tension relationship, is independent of innervation and contractility, and matches cardiac output to venous return — cardiac output can double without increasing heart rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Frank-Starling mechanism (the normal cardiac function curve) is based on the length-tension relationship, is independent of innervation and independent of contractility changes, and matches cardiac output to venous return — remarkably, cardiac output can double without any increase in heart rate, simply from increased filling (preload). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"In vectorial analysis of the EKG, what does the direction of current flow in the heart represent, and what is the approximate normal mean QRS vector angle?","choices":["Current flow direction reverses randomly with each heartbeat, so no consistent mean vector can ever be defined","The mean QRS vector angle has no clinical relevance, since axis deviation never reflects underlying structural disease","Current flows from the area of depolarization to polarized areas (− to +); the normal mean QRS vector is about 59 degrees","Current flows from polarized to depolarized areas (+ to −); the normal mean QRS vector is about 0 degrees"],"answer":2,"exp":"In the heart, current flows from the area of depolarization to polarized areas (from − to +), and the resulting potential can be represented as an instantaneous mean vector; the normal mean QRS vector is approximately 59 degrees. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Why is the T wave normally positive, given that repolarization proceeds in the opposite spatial sequence from depolarization?","choices":["Because depolarization and repolarization always occur in the identical spatial sequence, with the septum both first to depolarize and first to repolarize","The T wave has no relationship to repolarization sequence, being generated instead by residual current from the preceding QRS complex","Because the last areas to depolarize (outer ventricular surface) are the first to repolarize, so repolarized areas develop a positive charge first, producing a net positive vector","T waves are always negative in a normal heart, since repolarized tissue develops a net negative charge relative to depolarized tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"The septum/endocardium are the first areas to depolarize but the last to repolarize (longer contraction duration), while the outer ventricular surface is the last to depolarize but the first to repolarize; because the first areas to repolarize develop a positive charge first, a net positive vector is generated, producing a normally positive T wave. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the functional purpose of the atrial and ventricular syncytia acting together with contraction and relaxation?","choices":["To prevent any blood flow through the heart","To move blood in a single direction into and out of the heart","To generate the QRS complex only, with no mechanical function","To keep atrial and ventricular pressures always equal"],"answer":1,"exp":"The two pumps of the heart (right, sending blood to the lungs; left, sending blood to peripheral organs) both contract and relax in a coordinated way via their syncytia to move blood through the heart in one direction. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"How does the functional role of the right heart differ from the left heart?","choices":["Neither side generates any pressure","Both sides function identically as pressure pumps","The right heart is a volume pump; the left heart is a pressure pump","The right heart is a pressure pump; the left heart is a volume pump"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right heart functions primarily as a volume pump (moving the entire cardiac output to the lungs at low pressure), while the left heart functions as a pressure pump (generating the higher pressures needed for systemic circulation). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which ryanodine receptor isoform is found in cardiac muscle, versus skeletal muscle?","choices":["Neither muscle type expresses a ryanodine receptor","Both muscle types use an identical ryanodine receptor isoform","Cardiac muscle uses RYR-2; skeletal muscle uses RYR-1","Cardiac muscle uses RYR-1; skeletal muscle uses RYR-2"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cardiac muscle expresses the RYR-2 ryanodine receptor isoform (Ca2+-gated, opened by calcium-activated calcium release), while skeletal muscle expresses RYR-1 (voltage-gated, opened via the DHP receptor). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"In skeletal muscle, Ca2+ release is proportional to what variable, versus in cardiac muscle?","choices":["Cardiac muscle Ca2+ release is proportional to the frequency of action potentials; skeletal muscle Ca2+ release is proportional to extracellular Ca2+ entry through L-type channels","Both muscle types show SR Ca2+ release that is proportional entirely to the amount of extracellular Ca2+ entry through voltage-gated L-type calcium channels","In skeletal muscle, Ca2+ release is proportional to the frequency of action potentials; in cardiac muscle, it is proportional to the amount of Ca2+ entry through the L-type channel","Neither muscle type shows any proportional relationship between calcium release and either action potential frequency or Ca2+ channel activity"],"answer":2,"exp":"In skeletal muscle, SR Ca2+ release is proportional to the frequency of action potentials (voltage-activated calcium release); in cardiac muscle, SR Ca2+ release is proportional to the amount of Ca2+ entering through the L-type channel (calcium-activated calcium release). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which type of channel opens uniquely in cardiac (but not skeletal) muscle action potentials, producing the plateau phase?","choices":["Fast sodium channels only, which also produce the brief 1-2 ms depolarization typical of skeletal muscle","Only potassium channels, which uniquely produce a hyperpolarizing plateau not seen in skeletal muscle","No channel type differs between the two muscle types, since both rely exclusively on fast sodium channels throughout","Slow calcium-sodium channels (L-type Ca2+ channels), which also produce prolonged depolarization"],"answer":3,"exp":"While both skeletal and cardiac muscle open fast sodium channels during the AP upstroke, only cardiac muscle additionally opens slow calcium-sodium (L-type Ca2+) channels, producing the prolonged depolarization plateau not seen in skeletal muscle. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"If EDV is 120 mL and ESV is 20 mL, what are the stroke volume and ejection fraction?","choices":["SV = 140 mL, EF ≈ 1.2","SV = 100 mL, EF ≈ 0.8","SV = 120 mL, EF = 1.0","SV = 20 mL, EF ≈ 0.17"],"answer":1,"exp":"With EDV = 120 mL and ESV = 20 mL: SV = EDV − ESV = 100 mL, and EF = SV/EDV = 100/120 ≈ 0.8 (80%). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Cardiac glycosides like digoxin and digitalis have what type of inotropic effect, and via what mechanism related to calcium?","choices":["No effect on contractility whatsoever, acting instead solely to slow conduction through the A-V node","They only affect heart rate, slowing SA node discharge without altering contractile force at all","A negative inotropic effect, decreasing contractility by actively lowering intracellular calcium concentration","A positive inotropic effect, increasing contractility, associated with increased intracellular calcium concentration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cardiac glycosides (digoxin, digitalis) produce a positive inotropic effect, increasing contractility, associated with an increase in intracellular calcium concentration available for cross-bridge cycling. — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What are the typical normal values for systolic and diastolic aortic pressure?","choices":["Systolic 60 mmHg, diastolic 40 mmHg","Systolic 80 mmHg, diastolic 120 mmHg","Systolic 120 mmHg, diastolic 80 mmHg","Systolic and diastolic pressures are always equal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Typical normal aortic pressure values are a systolic pressure of about 120 mmHg and a diastolic pressure of about 80 mmHg, as reflected in the aortic pressure curve alongside the incisura (dicrotic notch). — 8. Cardiac Physiology.pptx, Slide 21"}]};
